'''''Hyacinthoides'' ×&nbsp;''massartiana''''' is a [[hybrid species]] produced by crosses between the common bluebell, ''[[Hyacinthoides non-scripta|H.&nbsp;non-scripta]]'' and the Spanish bluebell, ''[[Hyacinthoides hispanica|H.&nbsp;hispanica]]''. ''H.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''massartiana'' fills a spectrum of variation which connects the two parental species.<ref name="Stace">{{cite book |author=Clive A. Stace |year=2010 |title=New Flora of the British Isles |url=https://archive.org/details/newflorabritishi00stac |url-access=limited |edition=3rd |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]] |location=Cambridge |isbn=978-0-521-70772-5 |chapter=''Hyacinthoides'' Heist. ''ex'' Fabr. (''Endymion'' Dumort.) – bluebells |pages=[https://archive.org/details/newflorabritishi00stac/page/n955 920]–921|author-link=Clive A. Stace }}</ref>

== Distribution ==
''Hyacinthoides'' × ''massartiana'' has become widespread in Britain and Belgium,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Verloove|first=Filip|date=5 March 2018|title=Hyacinthoides x massartiana|url=http://alienplantsbelgium.be/content/hyacinthoides-x-massartiana|website=Manual of the Alien Plants of Belgium}}</ref> both of which have large populations of ''H. non-scripta''. It is often found on the edges of woodlands and roadsides, particularly in urban areas, which suggests that it has spread from gardens planted with ''H. hispanica''.<ref>{{Cite web|date=30 August 2010|title=Hyacinthoides non-scripta, H. hispanica & H. x massartiana|url=http://sppaccounts.bsbi.org/content/hyacinthoides-non-scripta-h-hispanica-h-x-massartiana.html|website=Botanical Society of Britain and Ireland: BSBI Species Accounts Archive}}</ref> There is great concern over the effect that this hybrid may have on native ''H. non-scripta'' populations, as it could dilute their characteristics and outcompete them  due to ''H. hispanica''<nowiki/>'s robust fertility and the effects of [[climate change]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hyslop|first=Sally|date=21 May 2015|title=Citizen Science Blog: Native and non-native {{!}} Bluebell survey|url=https://www.nhm.ac.uk/natureplus/community/citizen_science/blog/2015/05/21/native-and-non-native-bluebell-survey.html|website=Natural History Museum}}</ref>

==Taxonomic history==
Hybrids between ''H.&nbsp;non-scripta'' and ''H.&nbsp;hispanica'' were first given a [[specific name (botany)|specific name]] in 1997, when the Belgian botanist D. Geerinck described them as ''H.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''massartiana'',<ref>{{cite journal |author=D. Geerinck |year=1997 |orig-date=1996 |title=Une épithète pour l'hybride ''Hyacinthoides hispanica'' (Mill.) Rothm. × ''H. non-scripta'' (L.) Chouard ex Rothm.: ''H. × massartiana'' Geerinck (Liliaceae) |journal=[[Belgian Journal of Botany]] |volume=129 |issue=1 |pages=83–85 |jstor=20794380}}</ref> honouring the botanist [[Jean Massart]].<ref name="Clement">{{cite journal|author=Eric J. Clement |year=2000 |title=The hybrid bluebell, ''H.''&nbsp;×&nbsp;''massartiana'' |journal=BSBI News |publisher=[[Botanical Society of the British Isles]] |volume=84 |page=24 |url=http://www.watsonia.org.uk/BSBINews84.pdf }}{{dead link|date=April 2017 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> The [[type locality (biology)|type locality]] is [[Watermael-Boitsfort]], near [[Brussels]], [[Belgium]]; the [[holotype]] is held in Brussels, with an [[Isotype (biology)|isotype]] in [[Liège]].<ref name="Grundmann">{{cite journal |author1=Michael Grundmann |author2=Fred J. Rumsey |author3=Stephen W. Ansell |author4=Stephen J. Russell |author5=Sarah C. Darwin |author6=Johannes C. Vogel |author7=Mark Spencer |author8=Jane Squirrell |author9=Peter M. Hollingsworth |author10=Santiago Ortiz |author11=Harald Schneider |year=2010 |title=Phylogeny and taxonomy of the bluebell genus ''Hyacinthoides'', Asparagaceae &#91;Hyacinthaceae&#93; |journal=[[Taxon (journal)|Taxon]] |volume=59 |issue=1 |pages=68–82 |doi=10.1002/tax.591008 }}</ref> The same taxon had already been given the name "''Hyacinthoides'' ×&nbsp;''variabilis''" by P. D. Sell in 1996 in the ''Flora of Great Britain and Ireland'',<ref>{{cite book |author1=P. D. Sell |author2=G. Murrell |year=1996 |series=Flora of Great Britain and Ireland |volume=5 |title=Butomaceae–Orchidaceae |chapter=Liliaceae |pages=251–294 |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]] |location=Cambridge |isbn=978-0-521-55339-1}}</ref> but without a valid Latin diagnosis.<ref name="Clement"/>

